/// Chapter 45 of the guide, written from md-docs/45-upgrading.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page UpgradingGuide(path: "/docs/guide/upgrading", title: "Upgrading", description: "Installing a new version, what changed in 4.1, 4.0.1 and 4.0, and moving a WebFluent 2 or 3 project forward with wf migrate.", layout: DocsShell("Upgrading", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "45" }), blurb: "What changed in each release, what to do when you upgrade, and how wf migrate carries an older project forward.", source: "md-docs/45-upgrading.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "installing-a-newer-version", label: "Installing a newer version"), Anchor(id: "4-1-next-release", label: "4.1 (next release)"), Anchor(id: "4-0", label: "4.0"), Anchor(id: "3-x", label: "3.x"), Anchor(id: "from-webfluent-2", label: "From WebFluent 2")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/troubleshooting", prevLabel: "44. {t("ch.44")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/browser-support", nextLabel: "46. {t("ch.46")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Heading("Installing a newer version", id: "installing-a-newer-version").h2

        Markdown("Run the installer again — it replaces the binary in place — or `cargo install webfluent --force`. `wf --version` says what you have. Update `wf-lsp` and the editor extension with it: the language server is built from the same compiler.\n\nThe full notes for every release are in [`RELEASE_NOTES.md`](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/blob/master/RELEASE_NOTES.md); what follows is what an upgrade asks of you.")

        Heading("4.1 (next release)", id: "4-1-next-release").h2

        Markdown("Nothing to change in your code. New:\n\n- **Offline**: name `offline` in the config for a service worker, writes kept for later, and an update flow ([chapter 19](/docs/guide/offline)).\n- **Peers**: `peer link = rtc(signal: …)`, a WebRTC data channel to another page ([chapter 18](/docs/guide/realtime#a-peer)).\n- **`env` from a `.env` file and the shell**, on top of the config ([chapter 30](/docs/guide/environments)).\n- **Unknown config keys are reported**, with the key they probably meant.\n- **Pagination**: `paginate: .page`, `.items`, `.loadMore()`, `.hasMore`.\n- **`?lang=` opens a page in that locale**, as the `hreflang` alternates promise.\n- **`class`-exported libraries work through `external`**: a class is constructed when called.\n- **Every standard DOM event** is accepted in `on …`.\n\nFixed, and worth knowing because they could have bitten you:\n\n- **A private `env` value no longer reaches the bundle.** 4.0 refused a page that *read* a private name, but still wrote the whole `env` map into `app.js`. If you deployed 4.0 or 4.0.1 with a secret in `env`, rotate it.\n- An action's parameters are its own: `action move(by: Number)` read `by` as a signal and threw.\n- `head \{ \}` tags may read a page's `derived` values.\n- A resource's `state`, `data` and `error`, and a socket's `messages`, show their values in text.\n- `navigator` and `location` compile as the browser's.\n- Two declarations on one line — `theme T \{ color-primary: #0F766E  radius-md: 14px \}` — are two, not one.\n- `S04` reports two pages on one route; `A12` counts an `h1` in each branch of an `if` as one.")

        Heading("4.0", id: "4-0").h2

        Markdown("4.0 changed what the compiler allows. `wf migrate` handles the mechanical part and names every place that needs a decision:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf migrate --check"),
                Line(command: "wf migrate")
            ])

        Markdown("- **`env` is public or private.** A page may only read a name beginning `PUBLIC_` or listed in `public_env`. The migration adds every name your pages already read to `public_env` and prints the list: read it, because public means anyone who opens the site can read the value.\n- **An `on*` attribute is an error** (`onclick: \"…\"`); write `on click \{ \}`.\n- **A URL a browser would run is refused** — `javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`, `blob:`, `file:` in `href:`, `src:`, `to:`, `poster:` or `navigate()`.\n- **Stores are built on first read**, not at boot. A store whose set-up must run anyway takes `eager: true`.\n- **A `persist` value follows other tabs.** `sync: false` opts out.\n- **`WF.store` and `WF.host` were renamed** in the runtime surface.\n- **Layouts stay as written**: a `Row` no longer turns into a column on a narrow screen by itself. The migration adds `.stacks` to keep the old behaviour.")

        Heading("3.x", id: "3-x").h2

        Markdown("WebFluent 3 introduced the grammar this guide describes: lowercase declarations, one positional argument, flags after a dot, `on click \{ \}` handlers, `style \{ \}` blocks of CSS.")

        Heading("From WebFluent 2", id: "from-webfluent-2").h2

        Markdown("A WebFluent 2 file (`Page Home (path: \"/\") \{ … \}`) is refused by `wf build` with a pointer to the migration. `wf migrate` rewrites every `.wf` under `src/` into the current grammar — a change of spelling that builds to what it built before — and `wf migrate --wfx` writes the result in the indented layout.")
    }
}
